\par\pard\plain \li540\fi-270\sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch \ls1\ilvl0{\listtext	\f0\fs24\b0 \uc0\u8226 	}{\f0\fs24\b1 Industrially produced}{\f0\fs24\b0 : Lithography allowed thousands of identical prints to be made cheaply.}
\par\plain \ilvl0{\listtext	\f0\fs24\b0 \uc0\u8226 	}{\f0\fs24\b1 Prints with just a few painted strokes}{\f0\fs24\b0 : Many chromolithographs were sold in black and white, with the buyer adding a small amount of color, or the printer adding minimal hand\_coloring.}
\par\plain \ilvl0{\listtext	\f0\fs24\b0 \uc0\u8226 	}{\f0\fs24\b1 1800s context}{\f0\fs24\b0 : Chromolithography emerged in the 1850s and peaked in the late 19th century, making it the dominant method for affordable color art in homes and public spaces.}
\par\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0
\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f3\fs20\b1\i0 Chromolithography}{\f4\fs20\b0\i0  was a color printing technique that became popular in the 19th century, especially in the latter half, and was widely used for decorative and commercial art }
\par\plain \f4\fs20\b0\i0
\par\plain {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://exhibits.lib.byu.edu/victorianillustrators/1.html"}{\fldrslt\f4\fs20\b0\i0\ul\ulc0\cf4 exhibits.lib.byu.edu}}{\f4\fs20\b0\i0 . The process began with a black\_and\_white lithographic print, then added separate plates for each color. In the \loch\af4\hich\af4\dbch\af4\uc1\u8220\'93industrial\u8221\'94 or mass\_produced versions, the print was mostly complete, with only a few strokes of color applied by hand \hich\f4 \emdash \loch\f4  often by the buyer or a decorator \hich\f4 \emdash \loch\f4  to give it a finished look }
\par\plain \f4\fs20\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f4\fs20\b0\i0 \line }{\f0\fs24\b0 Much of the art from this time reflected the Dutch work ethic and spirit of independence. Following the 1581 Act of Abjuration, which declared independence from }{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://theartbog.com/art-history-of-spain-altamira-to-avant-garde/"}{\fldrslt\f0\fs24\b0 Spanish}}{\f0\fs24\b0  rule, the Dutch Republic entered a golden era, both economically and culturally. Urban centers like }{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://theartbog.com/amsterdam-the-history-of-its-art/"}{\fldrslt\f0\fs24\b0 Amsterdam}}{\f0\fs24\b0 , Haarlem, and Delft became hubs of innovation, attracting painters, merchants, and scholars alike. Wealthy merchants and local guilds became major patrons, shifting artistic production away from the Church and aristocracy.}
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 This shift gave artists an unusual level of creative freedom. Free from religious commissions, they could explore personal interests or cater to the desires of everyday citizens. The Dutch middle class valued realistic depictions of their lives\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 fine silverware, intimate home scenes, quiet landscapes, and expressive faces. This appetite for realism launched one of the most prolific and technically accomplished periods in European art history.}}